  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>Iran's Supreme National Security Council, through Secretary Mohsen Rezaei, gave a "within days" declaration for a formal Hormuz maritime exclusion zone on September 7 (GlobalSecurity.org, IranWarLive). A Tasnim News report the same day cited an unnamed IRGC naval commander as having completed "comprehensive maritime defensive measures" — a preparation-stage description, not a formal communique. Through EOD September 9, no IRNA, Tasnim, or PressTV official communique announcing the zone had appeared, and NOTAM databases showed no Iranian maritime restriction advisory. Day 2 of the announcement window has now elapsed without publication. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>FM spokesperson Baghaei described the Iran-Oman transit corridor arrangement as being in "final stages" on September 7, outlining terms of a 7-nautical-mile-wide lane, 60-day initial duration, and IMO registration (Bloomberg, Rigzone). Oman's FM Albusaidi had not published a parallel or joint statement as of September 9. No IMO Circular or NAVTEX for Hormuz corridor coordinates appeared. The IRGC's September 5-6 attacks on commercial vessels in what Iran termed the "unauthorized route" remained on record and without any Iranian commitment to halt. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>

      <p>The active bilateral exchange continued through the September 7-9 window. CENTCOM's September 5 tanker strikes and Iran's earlier September 5 ballistic missile launches at a US carrier strike group were not followed by additional reported strikes through September 9, per monitoring of US DoD and CENTCOM official channels — though this absence of new strikes over 4 days does not satisfy the 72h bilateral halt indicator, which requires both sides to stand down explicitly. Iran FM Araghchi's August 19 statement ("Iran rejects ceasefire") remained the most recent named-official Iranian diplomatic position through September 9. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>AIS tracker readings for September 7-8 continued to diverge beyond the 30% threshold established in <span class="cite">F7</span>'s ambiguity rule: Kpler recorded 11-12 commercial transits per day through Hormuz; TankerMap continued to report approximately 20-21/day. No additional TIER1 corroboration resolved the discrepancy. Brent crude front-month settled approximately $95.80 on September 8 — a marginal decline from $96.15 on September 7 following Baghaei's corridor-deal statement (Bloomberg) — leaving the 7-day rolling average approximately $7/barrel above <span class="cite">F8</span>'s YES threshold of $89.99. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span>, <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>No Israeli election polling data appeared from Jerusalem Post, Haaretz, Walla, Maariv, or Kan in the September 7-9 window. The September 4 Maariv/Lazar poll (Yashar 23, Likud 20) remained the most recent available data point. No Likud-bloc pre-election alliance announcement was issued in the same period. → supports <span class="cite">F5</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
