      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> F2 (exclusion zone, 58%) is the dominant near-term escalation trigger — a formal zone declaration before Sep 11 would formalize Iranian enforcement authority and risk direct CENTCOM confrontation in the strait.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Both F6 (corridor, 38%) and F3 (session, 8%) resolving NO leaves the tanker war frozen at current intensity — IRGC attacks on unauthorized vessels, US counter-strikes on Iranian assets, Hormuz at 5-15% normal traffic — with no diplomatic exit in the Sep 17 window.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Joint probability of F6 YES and F3 YES is approximately 3% (0.38 × 0.08) — requiring both a signed corridor deal and an Iran-US negotiating session within 8 days; the corridor alone (F6 YES) is the lower-threshold partial de-escalation signal with a 38% probability.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>Iranian state media framing on September 7-9 centered on three distinct narratives. IRNA and Tasnim presented Rezaei's exclusion zone signal as a sovereign rights exercise under international maritime law, placing it within the framework of what they described as Iran's "inherent right to regulate the Strait" — a legal framing absent from English-language IRGC statements and consistent with pre-announcement bureaucratic preparation. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>Press TV ran analysis claiming the IRGC held back from "maximum response" in the September 5 missile exchange, attributing the characterization to an unnamed Defense Ministry official. Domestically, this framing serves dual purposes: it prepares audiences for a potential step-up while providing post-hoc rationale for the decision not to escalate further in that exchange. No state media outlet published coverage of the Oman corridor negotiations, and Baghaei's September 7 "final stages" statement received minimal domestic amplification on IRNA or PressTV. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan ran its standard "America will regret" editorial framework, while Tasnim published analytical commentary claiming the September 5 US tanker strikes had "crossed a red line that Iran cannot ignore" — editorial framing from the outlet itself, not attributed to any official. IRIB news bulletins on September 8-9 led with the exclusion zone preparation narrative and made no reference to ceasefire or negotiation. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

